{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794952682,"nonce":"Mjeb/xnl5Rcl64MmJvurrbu8TPc=","message":"Checkpoint: 8576bc09090cbb2a0492625a082ab867f521e551 — perf(render): retain animated paint and reduce calendar bookkeeping\n\nStatus: OPEN. This checkpoint reduces CPU work and transient allocations; it does not establish a fix for the reported peak usage or pause.\n\nReproduction\n1. Build/run cn_demo with the release-debug profile (optimized), package blinc_app_examples, example cn_demo, features cn. Use two Cargo jobs; finish compilation before profiling.\n2. Run with BLINC_NODE_TABLE=0, BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=0, RUST_LOG=warn and BLINC_MOTION_TRACE unset, matching the measured native configuration.\n3. Scroll to Calendar. Use the left, unbounded Calendar, keeping the pointer over its day grid.\n4. On an Apple Magic Mouse, swipe with one finger rapidly and continuously in ONE direction for roughly 40 seconds. Alternating/slow drags are not the reported workload; the right Calendar's year bounds also make it unsuitable for the long comparison.\n\nActual and expected behaviour\nThe owner still observed CPU peaks up to 85% on the previous verified dd1dad57 native candidate. A 40-second capture on that build averaged 45.9938% process CPU (18.4 CPU seconds / 40.005364 wall seconds), yet the owner confirmed a pause followed by recovery during that same recording. A later attempt could not reproduce the pause, so whether the entire window or only Calendar movement stalled remains unclassified. Lower averages are not an acceptance criterion.\nExpected: responsive continuous swiping without transient stalls, preserved animation/clip/text fidelity, and CPU work limited to content that actually changes. No arbitrary FPS cap or reduced animation fidelity should be used to mask the cost.\n\nWhat this checkpoint contains\nRetained reactive translations, CSS group fades and solid background updates on CPU/GPU rows; consistent text snapping and inner clips; conservative repaint fallbacks; unchanged region-poll retention; selector metadata cleanup when rows leave; claimed-swipe descendant-hover suppression; native/headless CSS-layout parity; layout-rule prefiltering/borrowed styles; borrowed child traversal and carried culling offsets; focused regressions and tail/structure probes. A committed month still creates its day-grid region; there is no root UI rebuild on every animation frame.\n\nVerified evidence and limits\n- Four alternating 200-turn comparisons isolate the latest traversal change against the preceding CSS-layout candidate. Timing tails overlap; do not treat them as a native peak fix.\n- Matched 200/400-turn instruction slope: 38,901,855 -\u003e 35,926,960 retired instructions per simulated step, a 7.647% reduction. This is headless CPU work, not a 7.647-point drop in native CPU percentage.\n- 1,000 rapid month turns / 12,000 simulated steps: zero root builds, scope replacements or stale Calendar pages; selector-index peak stays 2490. Step p99 23.63 ms and max 92.69 ms. Keep the outlier visible in follow-up work.\n- Scoped Layout/region/culling tests, native/headless parity, retained-paint/Canvas/culling/Calendar tests on CPU and GPU rows, six exact RGBA comparisons, scoped Clippy (existing warnings), formatting/whitespace and optimized native build passed. All 56 committed source blobs match the validated manifest.\n- The latest native candidate is 2bfd9ead. Its native CPU peaks have NOT yet been validated. Memory peaks vary between runs; no controlled RSS reduction is claimed.\n- Aggregate profiles include bounds/culling, child-list/paint traversal and hit-testing work. Drawable waits also occur during normal vsync and are not a proven pause cause. Idle profiler intervals and an idle automatic peak watcher are explicitly non-evidence for active-swipe performance.\n\nRemaining validation\nA guarded native replay is prepared from 3748 logged application-delivered scroll events over 51.972 seconds, preserving the recorded deltas, positions and phase values. It uses the SDK's non-variadic CoreGraphics event constructor; local Cocoa roundtrip checks preserve pixel deltas and phases. Actual delivery/month advancement is still unverified because the Mac session was observed locked. The driver aborts while locked and posts only to an explicitly guarded owned PID/window. Logged timestamps/coordinates are not raw HID data.\nOnce the session is active, verify replay delivery with input logs and screenshots, then replay the same workload against the preserved old/new native binaries. Capture native peaks and frame/input timing around an actual pause; do not substitute another average-only conclusion. Keep this issue open until that evidence supports resolution.\n\nLocal evidence (ignored artifacts in this workspace)\n- target/calendar-probe/2026-11-17-native-input/cull-walk-ab/validation.json, matching-ab.json, instructions.json, source-after-sha256.json, source snapshots and gate logs.\n- .../layout-pass-ab/native-run-1/report.json and native.sample.txt/native.demangled.txt: owner-confirmed pause and 45.9938% average.\n- .../cull-walk-ab/native-replay/: guarded scripts, recorded-input.json, local-roundtrip.json and capability.json. Older calibration helpers contain stale PID/SHA guards; use fresh window metadata before any replay.\n- Preserved native binaries: /private/tmp/blinc-calendar-before-cull-native (dd1dad57) and /private/tmp/blinc-calendar-cull-native (2bfd9ead).\n\nNo tracker push, source push or issue closure accompanies this checkpoint.","files":null}]}